  "before_interfaces": {
    "alg-linear-elasticity-rigid-body-nullspace-gauge": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $K\\in\\mathbb R^{N\\times N}$ be symmetric positive semidefinite, let $R\\in\\mathbb R^{N\\times r}$ have full column rank and satisfy $\\ker K=\\operatorname{range}R$, and let $f\\in\\mathbb R^N$ be compatible in the sense $R^Tf=0$. Define the gauge solve as the procedure:\n\n1. Form the square augmented matrix and right-hand side\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}K&R\\\\R^T&0\\end{bmatrix},\\qquad \\begin{bmatrix}f\\\\0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n2. Solve for $(u,\\gamma)\\in\\mathbb R^N\\times\\mathbb R^r$.\n3. Return $u$, which is the representative satisfying $R^Tu=0$.\n\nThe input hypotheses must be checked for the particular stiffness matrix. The procedure does not identify an omitted nullspace or assert a load is compatible."
    ],
    "lem-linear-elasticity-rigid-body-gauge-system-is-nonsingular": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $K$ and $R$ satisfy the input hypotheses of [[alg-linear-elasticity-rigid-body-nullspace-gauge]]. Then $\\begin{bmatrix}K&R\\\\R^T&0\\end{bmatrix}$ is nonsingular. For $R^Tf=0$, its unique solution with right-hand side $\\begin{bmatrix}f\\\\0\\end{bmatrix}$ has $\\gamma=0$ and is the unique $u$ satisfying $Ku=f$ and $R^Tu=0$."
    ],
    "def-linear-elasticity-displacement-formulation-and-data": [
      "## Definition\n\nAssume Countable Choice. Let $d\\in\\{2,3\\}$ and let $D\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be nonempty, bounded, connected, and Lipschitz. Use the real spaces $V=H^1_0(D;\\mathbb R^d)$ and $Q=L^2_0(D)$, norms $\\|v\\|_V=\\|\\nabla v\\|_{L^2(D)}$ and $\\|q\\|_Q=\\|q\\|_{L^2(D)}$, and $b(v,q)=-\\int_Dq\\,\\operatorname{div}v$ as in [[def-na-stokes-zero-mean-pressure-and-velocity-spaces]]. For a real normed space $X$, write $X'$ for its continuous real dual with operator norm $\\|F\\|_{X'}=\\sup_{\\|x\\|_X\\le1}|F(x)|$. Let $\\mu\\in L^\\infty(D;\\mathbb R)$ satisfy $0<\\mu_0\\le\\mu(x)\\le\\mu_1<\\infty$ almost everywhere, let $\\lambda\\ge0$ be a scalar, and let $f\\in V'$. For any $w,z\\in H^1(D;\\mathbb R^d)$ define $\\varepsilon(w)=(\\nabla w+\\nabla w^T)/2$ and\n$$a_\\mu(w,z)=2\\int_D\\mu\\,\\varepsilon(w):\\varepsilon(z),\\qquad \\mathcal E_\\lambda(w,z)=a_\\mu(w,z)+\\lambda\\int_D\\operatorname{div}w\\,\\operatorname{div}z,$$\nand $\\sigma_\\lambda(w)=2\\mu\\varepsilon(w)+\\lambda(\\operatorname{div}w)I_d$. The homogeneous-Dirichlet model restricts these forms to $V$, with displacement zero on the full boundary; an example may prescribe a different boundary partition but gains no well-posedness claim from this definition. This is a linear small-strain model; no nonlinear, injectivity, or contrast-independent estimate is asserted.\n\nCountable Choice is inherited through the imported Sobolev and Stokes-space conventions; this definition makes no arbitrary-index selection."
    ],
    "lem-linear-elasticity-mixed-pressure-gauge-is-well-defined": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice. Let $D\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be bounded and open, let $0<\\lambda<\\infty$, and let $u\\in H^1_0(D;\\mathbb R^d)$. Set $p=-\\lambda\\operatorname{div}u$. With $Q=L^2_0(D)$ as in [[def-na-stokes-zero-mean-pressure-and-velocity-spaces]], one has $p\\in Q$."
    ],
    "thm-linear-elasticity-displacement-wellposedness-and-galerkin-bound": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice and the data of [[def-linear-elasticity-displacement-formulation-and-data]]. For every finite $\\lambda\\ge0$ there is a unique $u\\in V$ such that $\\mathcal E_\\lambda(u,v)=f(v)$ for every $v\\in V$. It satisfies\n$$\n\\|u\\|_V\\le\\mu_0^{-1}\\|f\\|_{V'},\\qquad \\sqrt{\\mathcal E_\\lambda(u,u)}\\le\\mu_0^{-1/2}\\|f\\|_{V'}.\n$$\nFor every finite-dimensional conforming subspace $V_h\\subset V$, the Galerkin solution $u_h$ is unique and\n$$\n\\|u-u_h\\|_V\\le\\frac{2\\mu_1+d\\lambda}{\\mu_0}\\inf_{v_h\\in V_h}\\|u-v_h\\|_V.\n$$"
    ],
    "def-linear-elasticity-near-incompressible-mixed-mini-pair": [
      "## Definition\n\nAssume Countable Choice, as in the imported model and mini-element spaces. Use the model, spaces, and sign convention of [[def-linear-elasticity-displacement-formulation-and-data]]. For finite $\\lambda>0$, set $p=-\\lambda\\operatorname{div}u\\in Q$ and write the mixed equations\n$$\na_\\mu(u,v)+b(v,p)=f(v),\\qquad b(u,q)-\\lambda^{-1}(p,q)_{L^2(D)}=0\n$$\nfor all $v\\in V$ and $q\\in Q$. The pressure belongs to $Q$ by [[lem-linear-elasticity-mixed-pressure-gauge-is-well-defined]], which uses the zero-mean-divergence result. At the endpoint $\\lambda=\\infty$, define $\\lambda^{-1}=0$ and impose $b(u,q)=0$; this is the incompressible constraint. At $\\lambda=0$ use the primal displacement problem with $p=0$, not a mixed saddle system.\n\nFor a connected polygonal or polyhedral $D$ in dimension $d=2$ or $3$ with a conforming shape-regular affine simplicial mesh, the discrete pair $(V_h,Q_h)$ is exactly the P1-bubble/P1 mini-element pair of [[def-na-mini-element-p1-bubble-p1-spaces]]. The discrete mixed equations use the same $a_\\mu,b,\\lambda^{-1}$ and replace $V,Q$ by $V_h,Q_h$."
    ],
    "thm-nearly-incompressible-elasticity-mini-uniform-stability-and-error": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice and the model and mixed conventions of [[def-linear-elasticity-displacement-formulation-and-data]] and [[def-linear-elasticity-near-incompressible-mixed-mini-pair]]. Let $D$ be bounded, connected, and Lipschitz, $d\\in\\{2,3\\}$, $0<\\mu_0\\le\\mu\\le\\mu_1$, and $f\\in V'$. For every $\\lambda\\in[0,\\infty]$ the continuous problem has a unique solution; at $\\lambda=0$ use the primal problem and set $p_0=0$, while at $\\lambda=\\infty$ impose $\\operatorname{div}u=0$. If $D$ is polygonal or polyhedral and $\\{\\mathcal T_h\\}$ is any conforming shape-regular affine simplicial mesh family with a common shape-regularity bound, the mini-element discrete problem has a unique solution for every $h$ and every $\\lambda\\in[0,\\infty]$, with $p_{h,0}=0$. There is a constant $C$ depending only on $d,D,\\mu_0,\\mu_1$ and the common shape-regularity bound, but independent of $\\lambda$ and $h$, such that\n$$\\|u_\\lambda\\|_V+\\|p_\\lambda\\|_Q\\le C\\|f\\|_{V'},\\qquad \\|u_\\lambda-u_{h,\\lambda}\\|_V+\\|p_\\lambda-p_{h,\\lambda}\\|_Q\\le C\\inf_{v_h\\in V_h,\\,q_h\\in Q_h}\\bigl(\\|u_\\lambda-v_h\\|_V+\\|p_\\lambda-q_h\\|_Q\\bigr).$$\nAt $\\lambda=\\infty$ the constraint is $\\operatorname{div}u=0$. The estimate makes no heterogeneous-coefficient robustness claim beyond the displayed dependence."
    ],
    "ex-linear-elasticity-rigid-body-stiffness-nullspace": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $D\\subset\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\in\\{2,3\\}$, be a connected polygonal or polyhedral domain with a finite conforming affine simplicial mesh $\\mathcal T_h$ and no essential boundary constraints. Define the continuous P1 vector space $S_h=\\{v\\in C^0(\\overline D;\\mathbb R^d):v|_K\\text{ is affine for every }K\\in\\mathcal T_h\\}$ and its global nodal basis $(\\phi_i)_{i=1}^N$. For constants $\\mu>0$ and $\\lambda\\ge0$, set $\\varepsilon(v)=(\\nabla v+\\nabla v^T)/2$, $k(v,w)=2\\mu\\int_D\\varepsilon(v):\\varepsilon(w)+\\lambda\\int_D\\operatorname{div}v\\,\\operatorname{div}w$, and $K_{ij}=k(\\phi_j,\\phi_i)$. For every infinitesimal rigid motion $r(x)=a+\\Omega x$, with $a\\in\\mathbb R^d$ and $\\Omega^T=-\\Omega$, its nodal coefficient vector lies in $\\ker K$. Thus the range of any complete nullspace basis supplied to [[alg-linear-elasticity-rigid-body-nullspace-gauge]] must contain these translation and rotation vectors. This example does not claim they exhaust the nullspace on every mesh."
    ],
    "ex-linear-elasticity-cantilever-manufactured-shear": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice. On $D=(0,1)^2$, clamp the left edge $\\Gamma_D=\\{0\\}\\times(0,1)$ and prescribe traction on the other three open edges $\\Gamma_N$. Let $\\mu>0$ and $\\lambda\\ge0$ be constants and set $u(x,y)=(0,x)$. The stress $\\sigma_\\lambda(u)=2\\mu\\varepsilon(u)+\\lambda(\\operatorname{div}u)I_2$ is constant and satisfies $-\\operatorname{div}\\sigma_\\lambda(u)=0$, $u|_{\\Gamma_D}=0$, and $\\sigma_\\lambda(u)n=(0,\\mu)$ on the right edge, $(\\mu,0)$ on the top edge, and $(-\\mu,0)$ on the bottom edge. This is a manufactured linear-elasticity solution; no uniqueness result for partial Dirichlet data is asserted."
